  - [25] Under the parity condition, the sum of the absolute values of the coefficients of a Moody polynomial equals the absolute value of its value at $t=-1$, and that value depends only on the Burau representation at $t=-1$.
  - [25] Consequently the conclusion of Proposition 6.7 cannot hold for any braid $\Phi$ with $\rho_4(\Phi)\at=I$, and every element of $\ker\rho_4$ is such a braid.
  - [25] We give an explicit nontrivial Brunnian braid $\Phi_0$ with $\rho_4(\Phi_0)\at=I$.
  - [25] Either no conjugate of $\Phi_0$ is a proper product $\Phi'\cdot\Gamma_1$, and then the reduction step in the proof of Theorem 6.9 fails for $\Phi_0$, or some conjugate is, and then Proposition 6.7 fails for it; the criterion stated in the proof of Theorem 6.9 puts $\Phi_0$ in the second case.
  - [25] Inside the proof of Proposition 6.7 the problem can be located further: for such braids, if the loop $\Gamma$ constructed there has the parity properties the proof claims, then the modified loop $\Gamma'$ used for the final inequality cannot also have them, and the proof does not re-check them.
  + [26] Under the parity condition, the sum of the absolute values of the coefficients of a Moody polynomial equals the absolute value of its value at $t=-1$, and that value depends only on the braid's image under the Burau representation at $t=-1$.
  + [26] Consequently the conclusion of Proposition 6.7 cannot hold for any proper product $\Phi=\Phi'\cdot\Gamma_1$ with $\rho_4(\Phi)\at=I$.
  + [26] So if Proposition 6.7 and the reduction step in the proof of Theorem 6.9 were both correct, they would show that $\rho_4$ specialized at $t=-1$ is faithful on $\Brun_4$.
  + [26] That is false: we give an explicit nontrivial Brunnian braid $\Phi_0$ with $\rho_4(\Phi_0)\at=I$.
  + [26] Either no conjugate of $\Phi_0$ is a proper product $\Phi'\cdot\Gamma_1$, and then the reduction step fails for $\Phi_0$, or some conjugate is, and then Proposition 6.7 fails for it; the criterion stated in the proof of Theorem 6.9 puts $\Phi_0$ in the second case.
  + [26] Inside the proof of Proposition 6.7 the problem can be located further: for such braids, if the loop $\Gamma$ constructed there has the parity properties the proof claims, then the modified loop $\Gamma'$ cannot have them and also satisfy the final inequality, and the proof does not re-check them for $\Gamma'$.
