insert old lines [] new lines [53]
  + [53] Minimal position is used here only to identify $m$ with $\iota(\alpha,(\bs)\varphi)$.
  + [53] The Moody polynomial can be computed from any transverse representative (Section 2 of \cite{BBB}).
  + [53] For such a representative of $(\bs)\varphi$ whose disk sequence satisfies the parity condition, with $m'$ intersection points, the same argument gives $|\M_\varphi\at|=m'$, while a representative in minimal position gives $|\M_\varphi\at|\le\iota(\alpha,(\bs)\varphi)\le m'$.
  + [53] So such a representative is automatically in minimal position, and the lemma applies whichever representative the parity condition is checked on, without Proposition 5.1.
