  - [84] Since this condition is invariant under conjugation, steps (A) and (C) together would show that every nontrivial element of $\Brun_4$ is nontrivial under $\rho_4$ at $t=-1$.
  - [84] That statement is stronger than Theorem 6.9, and Section 4 shows that it is false.
  + [87] Since this condition is invariant under conjugation, steps (A) and (C) together would show that every nontrivial element of $\Brun_4$ is nontrivial under $\rho_4$ at $t=-1$, that is, that $\rho_4$ specialized at $t=-1$ is faithful on $\Brun_4$.
  + [87] That statement is stronger than Theorem 6.9, and Section 4 below shows that it is false.
  + [87] Proposition~\ref{prop:main} uses neither the proper-product hypothesis nor the loop $\Gamma_1$, so this argument applies unchanged if $\Gamma_1$ is replaced by any other loop in steps (A) and (C).
  + [87] Restricting step (C) to elements of $\ker\rho_4$ does not help either: if Proposition 6.7 held for all proper products $\Phi'\cdot\Gamma_1$, step (C) for the nontrivial elements of $\ker\rho_4\cap\Brun_4$ would be equivalent to Theorem 6.9 itself.
  + [87] The statement of Corollary 6.8 is consistent with Proposition~\ref{prop:main}, since two Laurent polynomials whose values at $t=-1$ agree up to sign can still differ; what Proposition~\ref{prop:main} rules out is the route to it through Proposition 6.7.
