replace old lines [138] new lines [143]
  - [138] The argument of Lemma~\ref{lem:tminus1}, together with the fact that $t+1$ and $1-t^{\pm1}$ are coprime in $\mathbb{Z}[t^{\pm1}]$, shows that $\M_{f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma}\equiv\M_\Gamma\pmod{(t+1)^d}$ when $\Phi$ has depth $\ge d$.
  - [138] If both braids satisfy the parity condition, Lemma~\ref{lem:sign} gives $\M(t)=\pm p(-t)$ with $p(s)=\sum_i s^{k_i}$, so the exponent multisets $\{k_i\}$ of $f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma$ and $\Gamma$ have the same factorial moments $p^{(j)}(1)$ for $j<d$; the comparison in Corollary 6.8 is the case $j=0$.
  + [143] The argument of Lemma~\ref{lem:tminus1}, together with the fact that $t+1$ is a prime element of $\mathbb{Z}[t^{\pm1}]$ that does not divide $1-t^{\pm1}$, shows that $\M_{f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma}\equiv\M_\Gamma\pmod{(t+1)^d}$ when $\Phi$ has depth $\ge d$.
  + [143] In particular, if $\M_{f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma}\neq\M_\Gamma$, the exponents of $\M_{f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma}-\M_\Gamma$ span at least $d$.
  + [143] If both braids satisfy the parity condition, Lemma~\ref{lem:sign} gives $\M(t)=\pm p(-t)$ with $p(s)=\sum_i s^{k_i}$, so the exponent multisets $\{k_i\}$ of $f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma$ and $\Gamma$ have the same factorial moments $p^{(j)}(1)$ for $j<d$ (the case $j=0$ also forces the two signs to agree); the comparison in Corollary 6.8 is the case $j=0$.
