replace old lines [152] new lines [157]
  - [152] We used $\sigma_i\mapsto I_{i-1}\oplus\begin{pmatrix}1-t&t\\1&0\end{pmatrix}\oplus I_{n-i-1}$, its transpose, the reduced Burau representation and its transpose, and the Artin action of $\B_n$ on the free group $F_n$ for triviality checks; $f$ is $\sigma_i\mapsto\sigma_i$.
  - [152] In each of these conventions:
  + [157] We used $\sigma_i\mapsto I_{i-1}\oplus\begin{pmatrix}1-t&t\\1&0\end{pmatrix}\oplus I_{n-i-1}$, the matrices of \cite[Section 4.2]{BB05}, to which Section 2 of \cite{BBB} refers for Burau's original definition.
  + [157] By Fox calculus these are the matrices of the action on $H$ in the basis of lifts of $x_1,\dots,x_n$, with $t$ the deck transformation corresponding to each $x_k$, when $\sigma_i$ acts by $x_i\mapsto x_ix_{i+1}x_i^{-1}$, $x_{i+1}\mapsto x_i$.
  + [157] We also used the transposed matrices, the reduced representation in which $\sigma_i$ replaces row $i$ of $I_{n-1}$ by $(t,-t,1)$ in columns $i-1,i,i+1$, and its transpose; conjugating this transpose by $\mathrm{diag}(1,-1,1,\dots)$ gives the reduced matrices of \cite[Section 4.2]{BB05}.
  + [157] Replacing every $\sigma_i$ by the opposite half twist, or $t$ by $t^{-1}$, changes all of these by $t\mapsto t^{-1}$ up to conjugation.
  + [157] This does not affect statements at $t=-1$ or about depth, and for the other evaluations we also use $t=1/2$ and $t=1/3$.
  + [157] Triviality is checked with the Artin action of $\B_n$ on the free group $F_n$, and $f$ is $\sigma_i\mapsto\sigma_i$.
  + [157] In each of the four matrix conventions:
